+\subsection{Implementation Profiles and Upstream Record Coverage}
+\label{subsec:formal-profile-closure}
+
+The preceding statements are conditional properties of named profiles, not a
+single certificate for every interface that exports a graph. The full round
+verifier reconstructs admission, scoring, masses and checkpoint bytes. A graph
+query authenticates its supplied view and paths; it does not independently
+rerun local training or every investigative product. A round-local analytical
+intervention likewise does not acquire fresh attestation merely by referring
+to an authenticated historical round. Historical signatures retain their
+original serialization and source release when later implementations change.
+
+Write $B_t$ for the incoming model of round $t$ and $\theta_t$ for its output.
+The original proposal denotes these by $W(t)$ and $W(t+1)$, whereas the later
+experiment records use $W_{t-1}$ and $W_t$. Thus a correction to experimental
+round 10 starts from $B_{10}=W_9$. These are index translations, not different
+execution orders. In this section $m_t(s)$ denotes unnormalized mass,
+$c_t(s)$ its policy factor, and $\alpha_t(s)$ the normalized coefficient.
+
+Submission identity includes context and client as well as bundle content.
+The corrected graph therefore identifies an update-bundle occurrence by its
+submission identity while retaining the original content hash as an attribute.
+Reusing identical bytes in a different client or round slot cannot acquire the
+original occurrence's numerical descendants. Conflicting signed claims can
+have separate negative dispositions while still occupying at most one numerical
+slot per client. Regression cases cover both cross-round replay and a
+same-round claim to another client's bundle. This correction concerns graph
+identity; it does not retrospectively re-sign earlier graphs.
+
+There is a separate upstream completeness obligation. For a named ingestion
+profile, let $I_0$ enumerate occurrences by committed source, position and record
+digest. Equal record values at different positions remain different inputs.
+The verifier requires exactly one terminal outcome per occurrence: a derived
+retained event/window association, or an identifiable discard with its reason.
+Many source occurrences may share a consolidated event or feature window.
+Verification re-enumerates the pinned sources, reruns the transformation and
+checks identities and associations rather than only matching totals. Blank
+lines, parsing failures and training-window selection have profile-specific
+outcomes; an unsupported transformation fails rather than receiving a fabricated
+successful coverage manifest. An empty input has only trivial coverage.
+
+The JSONL, Data24 CSV and Data24 Parquet profiles make this statement relative
+to their committed $I_0$. The new replay writes its inventory before its own
+transformation, but that ordering does not independently timestamp an earlier
+historical capture. It does not establish that all network events were captured
+before ingestion. Similarly, receipt-relative submission coverage does not
+establish delivery or a trusted registration deadline. An independently
+administered append-only witness with trusted closure and retained heads remains
+a stronger deployment hypothesis, not an implemented external service.
+
+\subsection{Fixed-Client Diagnostics and Their Failure Domain}
+\label{subsec:formal-fixed-client-diagnostics}
+
+Let $K$ be the fixed expected-client set and compare two successful round
+normalizations, padding absent coefficients with zero. The original measures are
+\begin{align}
+ \Delta_K &= \sum_{k\in K}|\alpha'_k-\alpha_k|,\\
+ J_K &= \mathbf{1}\{\exists k\in K:A'_k\ne A_k\}.
+ \label{eq:formal-fixed-diagnostics}
+\end{align}
+The augmented action alphabet includes an explicit \texttt{not\_submitted}
+outcome. For a removed set of original coefficient mass $\beta<1$, the removed
+coordinates contribute $\beta$ to $\Delta_K$, and the surviving coordinates
+contribute at least $\beta$ because their signed changes sum to $\beta$.
+Hence $\Delta_K\ge 2\beta$. Frozen-decision renormalization attains equality.
+These quantities are undefined when a required normalization is absent.
+
+Under the stated absence convention, every nonempty allowed removal already
+gives $J_K=1$. It therefore cannot by itself establish an indirect policy effect.
+The implementation separately reports the fraction of \emph{surviving} actions
+that change, survivor coefficient differences, and deviation beyond ordinary
+renormalization. These supplementary diagnostics do not replace the original
+fixed-$K$ definitions. Nor do scalar norms of direct and policy effects add in
+general: vector effects can reinforce or cancel.
+
+Semantic reviews remain in a separate authenticated append-only ledger and
+lineage index. Runtime graph traversal uses numerical, decision and audit edges;
+it is not presented as a unified four-edge traversal including semantic review.
+An externally retained expected head is needed to detect rollback relative to
+a known review history. A valid unanchored alternative head alone cannot
+establish the unique history or the truth of a reviewer's judgment.
+
+\subsection{Authenticated Containment of Derived Contributions}
+\label{subsec:formal-bounded-influence}
+
+An additional experimental profile transforms a contribution before aggregation.
+It retains the original signed model $U_{t,s}$ and records a separate derived
+object. For tensor $\ell$, with fixed base $B_t$ and a positive clean-reference
+limit $C_\ell$, define
+\begin{align}
+ d_{t,s,\ell} &= U_{t,s,\ell}-B_{t,\ell},\\
+ \eta_{t,s,\ell} &= \min\{1,C_\ell/\lVert d_{t,s,\ell}\rVert\},\\
+ v_{t,s,\ell} &= \eta_{t,s,\ell}d_{t,s,\ell},\\
+ \theta_{t,\ell} &= B_{t,\ell}+\sum_s\alpha_t(s)v_{t,s,\ell}.
+ \label{eq:formal-clipped-aggregation}
+\end{align}
+For a zero delta, $\eta=1$. The original signature is not transferred to the
+derived delta. The declared limits, original bundle/update hashes, factors,
+effective masses and float64 derived values are reconstructed independently;
+the coordinator's signed derivation manifest binds that transformation. The
+graph includes original bundle $\rightarrow$ bounded derivation $\rightarrow$
+checkpoint, with base and limit dependencies. The final checkpoint is exported
+to its declared tensor dtype.
+
+The original hard gate still determines admissibility. Failed gates and
+quarantine have zero mass, and insufficient quorum yields no checkpoint.
+Clipping never restores rejected mass. The newer statistical adapters expose
+proposals; unsupported-context abstention may retain eligible proposed mass.
+The live caller explicitly closes that domain by assigning zero final mass
+and producing a verified failure without a checkpoint. This conservative
+complete-cohort rule has an availability cost and does not claim calibrated
+operation on an arbitrary partial cohort.
+
+In exact arithmetic, $\lVert v_{t,s,\ell}\rVert\le C_\ell$. Convex normalization
+and the triangle inequality therefore imply
+\begin{equation}
+ \left\lVert\sum_s\alpha_t(s)v_{t,s,\ell}\right\rVert\le C_\ell.
+ \label{eq:formal-cap}
+\end{equation}
+Freeze the original inputs, base, limits and survivor decisions. Removing
+client $k$ with $\alpha_t(k)<1$ changes the tensor aggregate by
+$\alpha_t(k)(v_{t,k,\ell}-\mu_{-k,\ell})$, where $\mu_{-k,\ell}$ is the
+renormalized survivor mean. Both vectors have norm at most $C_\ell$, giving
+\begin{equation}
+ \lVert\theta_{t,\ell}-\theta_{t,-k,\ell}\rVert
+ \le 2\alpha_t(k)C_\ell.
+ \label{eq:formal-cap-removal}
+\end{equation}
+For a removed group of total coefficient mass $A<1$, the analogous bound is
+$2AC_\ell$. A real survivor checkpoint additionally requires survivor quorum.
+Otherwise the algebraic mean does not authorize a checkpoint rejected by the
+live policy. The numerical certificates include both checkpoint-export rounding
+errors and a declared $10^{-12}$ comparison allowance; they are finite checks,
+not a proof of arbitrary floating-point arithmetic.
+
+Here $\alpha_t(s)$ weights the \emph{derived} contribution. Its raw-delta
+representation has tensor-specific coefficient $\alpha_t(s)\eta_{t,s,\ell}$
+and base coefficient $1-\sum_s\alpha_t(s)\eta_{t,s,\ell}$. Because clipping is
+nonlinear, these observed algebraic coefficients are not marginal derivatives
+or demonstrated causal responsibility. They must not silently replace the
+original coefficients in $\Delta_K$. The norm bound establishes neither
+semantic malice nor an ASR guarantee, clean-state recovery or a bound on later
+retraining. The legacy raw-update counterfactual adapter explicitly rejects
+this profile; its clipped-delta removal operation is separate.
+
