+\section{Authenticated Multiround Evidence and Replay}
+\label{sec:revision-authenticated-replay}
+
+The earlier 466-test milestone in Section~\ref{sec:ch5-derivation-validation}
+is preserved as a historical validation of the small CPU replay and structural
+fixtures. A later, separate case exercises the complete signed round path with
+real Data24 local training and emulated TPMs. Its four five-round trajectories
+comprise an original DBA history, removal of client01's first submission,
+removal of that client's three attack-round submissions, and an unchanged
+attacked control. Client01 returns in later rounds; these are explicit
+submission schedules, not permanent identity bans. Clients06 and11 remain.
+
+The case has 300 fresh verified swtpm Quotes, 296 original local training
+outputs and 12 actual investigative bundles. Prediction, explanation, mapping
+and report bundles are produced for the original and two intervention branches.
+The separate numerical verification repeats all 296 local trainings under the
+frozen implementation. It adds no Quotes. The unchanged attacked control
+reproduces every checkpoint byte. These are twenty round instances over four
+trajectories, not a single twenty-round history or physical-TPM evaluation.
+
+The supplied graph contains 1,678 nodes and 4,757 edges. The selected first-round
+source and the union of its three attack-round submissions both reach 311
+dependent objects, including five checkpoint models and four product manifests.
+The earliest source can already reach later descendants, so equal union size
+does not imply equal intervention strength. Counts are relative to this supplied
+graph; no global registration of all possible products is established.
+
+The final model L2 distance from the original is 0.388520 for single-submission
+removal and 0.412999 for removal in rounds 1--3. Later local models are genuinely
+retrained from their own branch checkpoints, rather than reused from the
+original future. Nevertheless the complete-trigger ASR is zero in all four
+branches, including the original attack and unchanged attacked control.
+This case demonstrates authenticated propagation and reproducibility, not
+successful removal of an effective backdoor. The longer efficacy studies in
+Section~\ref{sec:revision-dba-groups} answer a different question.
+
+Nine attributed retrospective reviews bind the known scripted poisoning and
+its input commitments. Historical decisions, coefficients, checkpoints and
+products remain unchanged. Those judgments use experimental ground truth;
+they do not establish automatic identification of real malicious intent.
+The historical graph release remains preserved. A subsequent review corrects
+the occurrence-identity defect described in
+Section~\ref{subsec:formal-profile-closure}; the retained case itself has 296
+distinct submission origins, so that specific alias condition is absent there.
+
+\subsection{Source Versions and Evidence Types}
+\label{subsec:revision-source-versions}
+
+Source identity is part of the experiment, not merely a build annotation.
+The final correspondence review contains 633 passing tests at the corrected
+114-file release. The later empirical live adapters and containment profile
+have a 119-file release with 678 passing tests. The full-source fingerprint of
+the latter is
+\begin{quote}\small\ttfamily
+4b58e1590179dbaa37c8e9760b94bd57549db6c3a228e95f00ee2def26012bbb
+\end{quote}
+Old signed artifacts remain associated with their own original serializers,
+code and numerical environment. They are not migrated or re-signed to imply
+that a later release produced them. Function-body agreement is a local check,
+not proof of equivalence of all transitive dependencies. Unit-test totals,
+artifact hashes, actual training replay and manual conditional proofs provide
+different evidence and are not interchangeable.
+
+The theorem-to-code correspondence separates receipt/intake verification,
+full admission verification, graph reachability, ingestion coverage, review
+history and experiment-specific numerical replay. The logical conditions of
+the bound in Section~\ref{subsec:formal-bounded-influence} do not turn empirical
+calibration into proof of semantic trust. Analyst-signed closures bind study
+inputs, scripts and outputs under local software keys; they are not independent
+timestamp services, physical TPM signatures of the analysis or external
+mathematical certification.
