+\section{Distributed Backdoors, Literature Adaptations, and Retrospective Groups}
+\label{sec:revision-dba-groups}
+
+This section consolidates the added experiments motivated by the derivation
+model. It distinguishes shared-update comparisons, independently evolving
+trajectories, retrospective interventions and mechanism validation. A successful
+signature authenticates the submitted object; it does not establish a benign
+local objective. Conversely, an update can have recorded numerical influence
+without achieving its attack target. All study summaries retain negative and
+partial outcomes instead of converting them into successful defense results.
+
+\subsection{Distributed Trigger and Common-Input Comparison}
+\label{subsec:revision-dba-baselines}
+
+The Data24 DBA protocol has fifteen clients and three controlled adversaries.
+Each attacker edits a deterministic 50\% of its local training records, adds
+its own component at standardized feature index 13, 17 or 24, and assigns the
+target label \texttt{benign}. The complete evaluation trigger combines all
+three components; component evaluations preserve the true reference labels.
+The trigger value is 12 in the standardized feature space. This is a
+feature-space model experiment, not proof that such values can be realized in
+raw network traffic. A second condition applies all components to each selected
+row. Its row-edit budget matches DBA, but its feature-edit budget is larger.
+
+Nine source histories cover clean, distributed and complete-trigger training
+at seeds 341593, 342593 and 343593. Each has fifteen rounds, with attack at
+rounds 1--10 and unmodified continuations at rounds 11--15. Local Adam uses ten
+epochs, batch 128 and learning rate 0.001. The retained partitions, scaler and
+initial model are fixed. Server validation has 3,223 windows and the existing
+test fold 4,937. The source recurrence is FedAvg. Other methods reaggregate the
+same incoming base and local updates; their values are conditional round
+comparisons, not eight separate defense-controlled training histories.
+
+\begin{table}[tbp]
+\centering\small
+\caption[DBA and complete-trigger shared-update comparison]{Round-10 test outcomes on common inputs.
+ASRs are percentages, mean $\pm$ sample standard deviation over three seeds.
+The final column is clean macro-F1 under the complete-trigger condition.}
+\label{tab:revision-baselines}
+\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
+\toprule
+Method & DBA ASR & Complete ASR & Macro-F1 (\%) \\
+\midrule
+FedAvg & $44.09\pm3.75$ & $96.97\pm0.52$ & $91.57\pm0.89$ \\
+Coordinate median & $0.02\pm0.03$ & $86.57\pm3.18$ & $91.16\pm0.30$ \\
+Trimmed mean & $0.04\pm0.03$ & $89.70\pm2.12$ & $91.13\pm0.31$ \\
+Multi-Krum & $0.00\pm0.00$ & $63.69\pm8.73$ & $90.16\pm1.86$ \\
+Bulyan & $0.00\pm0.00$ & $68.05\pm1.60$ & $90.55\pm0.70$ \\
+FLAME adaptation & $0.00\pm0.00$ & $64.15\pm6.85$ & $89.01\pm2.05$ \\
+WeiDetect adaptation & $0.00\pm0.00$ & $95.09\pm1.84$ & $93.27\pm2.46$ \\
+Original composite & $0.00\pm0.00$ & $88.21\pm0.22$ & $91.90\pm0.55$ \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+The original composite suppresses the distributed trigger in these conditional
+outputs but retains substantial complete-trigger ASR. A poisoned incoming
+checkpoint can preserve a backdoor even when current poisoned updates are
+removed. At round 15, mean complete-trigger ASR remains 11.81\% for FedAvg,
+2.69\% for FLAME, 23.93\% for WeiDetect and 11.37\% for the original composite.
+Zero final DBA ASR also occurs in the continuing FedAvg source and does not
+prove a unique defense advantage or equality with a clean reference model.
+
+Operational selection is reported separately. During the 90 poisoned-update
+opportunities of DBA rounds 1--10, FLAME excludes 90, WeiDetect 67 and the
+original composite 90. Their currently clean exclusions are respectively
+111, 23 and 1 out of 360 opportunities; clipping and downweighting are separate
+operations. In the complete-trigger condition the corresponding poisoned
+exclusions are 90, 36 and 77, with 114, 54 and 1 currently clean exclusions.
+The old composite's zero strict exclusions on its own clean reference are an
+in-sample result. A secondary comparison with separate clean references gives
+15/675 IID and 16/675 non-IID clean exclusions. The individual-envelope policy
+has zero observed clean exclusions in that added comparison, but its larger
+clean-reference budget prevents an equal-information superiority claim.
+
+The implementation retains FLAME's clustering, clipping, averaging and noise
+stages~\cite{nguyen2022flame}, with a declared 0.01 noise factor. It is an
+MLP/Data24 adaptation, not the original experimental system or a new privacy
+guarantee. WeiDetect follows the retained v1 auxiliary-F1, Weibull and top-$K$
+procedure~\cite{sameera2025weidetectv1}: 2,238 correctly classified high-confidence
+initial examples form the fixed auxiliary set, and $K=12$ of fifteen forces
+three removals even in an all-clean round. Of 135 fits, 88 are degenerate and
+use the declared fallback. All rankings match the seeded F1 top-$K$ selection.
+Neither fixed pruning nor a fitting fallback establishes malicious intent.
+
+\subsection{Actual-Gradient FedGradTrace Adaptations}
+\label{subsec:revision-fedgradtrace}
+
+FedGradTrace's printed Eq.~(2) and Eq.~(3) imply different recurrences, and its
+positive trajectory-norm factors do not themselves implement the text's
+suggested suppression of large norms~\cite{gu2026fedxlog}. Both literal
+interpretations are therefore retained. The experiment collects actual
+full-snapshot class-weighted gradients at the current global base, with a
+three-snapshot trajectory and global SGD step 0.001. It does not substitute
+multi-epoch Adam model deltas for gradients. The 10\% poisoning fraction and
+gradient protocol differ from the 50\%/local-Adam DBA comparison; results are
+not pooled. The MLP adapter is not a reproduction of the full FedXLog system.
+
+The gradient-FedAvg and literal-Eq.~(3) cases complete ten clean and ten
+attacked rounds each. Their full-trigger ASR is zero even for the gradient
+control, so they do not demonstrate attack mitigation. Literal Eq.~(2) reduces
+final clean accuracy to 26.09\%. Its attacked execution completes only three
+of ten requested rounds: coefficient sum is approximately 10,247.55, clean
+accuracy 44.68\%, and both triggered and untriggered non-target examples map
+to the target at 100\%. The partial next state reaches parameter magnitude
+$1.37\times10^{30}$ and produces invalid validation losses. This is broad
+prediction collapse, not a selective stealth backdoor. No valid fourth-round
+or ten-round result is invented. All 53 completed aggregate exports replay
+exactly, with separately declared tolerance for floating diagnostics.
+
+\subsection{Measured Computational Scope}
+\label{subsec:revision-measured-cost}
+
+The audit measures 3,240 kernel calls and 162 full-scoring calls in sequential
+single-thread jobs over preloaded inputs. One warm-up per method/scope is
+excluded, and equivalence is checked outside the timer. Table~\ref{tab:revision-cost}
+keeps scopes explicit: cached kernel inputs do not represent full scoring.
+Process peak memory includes common Torch/Flower libraries and allocator state,
+not only incremental defense allocation. Loading, transfer, local training,
+reference generation, TPM and first imports lie outside these timers.
+
+\begin{table}[tbp]
+\centering\small
+\caption[Measured aggregation and scoring costs]{Wall time in milliseconds; median and 95th percentile.
+The envelope rows use different source contexts and cannot supply a paired speedup ratio.}
+\label{tab:revision-cost}
